---
import AdminLayout from '../../layouts/AdminLayout.astro';

const user = Astro.locals.user;
---

<AdminLayout title="System Documentation">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto">
        <div class="mb-10 border-b border-border pb-6">
            <h2 class="text-4xl font-black tracking-tight text-foreground uppercase">System Documentation</h2>
            <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mt-3 font-medium max-w-3xl">
                "Rigorous study of documentation and source code builds the load-bearing mental models required for resilient system design. Prioritizing immediate output over deep comprehension exchanges long-term stability for superficial efficiency."
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-8">
            <div class="lg:col-span-3 space-y-12">
                
                {/* 0. Critical System Rules & Database Environments */}
                <section id="docs-environments" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-red-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-red-500/80 group-hover:bg-red-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-red-500">Database Environments & Safety</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        
                        <div class="bg-destructive/10 border-l-4 border-destructive p-4 rounded-r-md">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-destructive mb-1">CRITICAL SYSTEM RULE: Database Preservation</h4>
                            <p class="text-sm text-destructive/90">
                                <strong>NEVER delete, wipe, or recreate the database folder (`.wrangler`) under any circumstances.</strong> 
                                The local database often serves as a carbon copy of the real WordPress database. Deleting it destroys hours of manual synchronization and cleanup. 
                                AI Agents are strictly forbidden from executing deletion commands or inserting arbitrary "dummy data" unless explicitly authorized by the user typing exactly: "Purge Development Database".
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="bg-muted/30 p-4 rounded-md border border-border mt-4">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Environment Topography</h4>
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
                                <li>
                                    <strong class="text-foreground">Local Development:</strong> Runs via Miniflare. The physical SQLite database files are stored inside the project folder at: 
                                    <code class="px-1 py-0.5 bg-background rounded border text-xs break-all">.wrangler/state/v3/d1/miniflare-D1DatabaseObject/</code>. 
                                    This database is completely isolated. Connect via `npm run dev` or local D1 commands like <code class="px-1 py-0.5 bg-background rounded text-xs">npx wrangler d1 execute DB --local --command="..."</code>.
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <strong class="text-foreground">Local Cloud Development (Preview):</strong> Uses a remote Cloudflare D1 database dedicated to staging/development. Connect by passing the `--remote` flag to wrangler, e.g., <code class="px-1 py-0.5 bg-background rounded text-xs">npm run dev --remote</code>.
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <strong class="text-foreground">Production:</strong> The live database hosted on Cloudflare's global edge network. Accessed only via deployed code or explicit `--remote` D1 commands tied to the production environment.
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="bg-muted/30 p-4 rounded-md border border-border mt-4">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Vendor Agnostic & Generic Adapter Pattern</h4>
                            <p class="mb-3">Waritaku CMS is fundamentally designed to be highly modular. By default, the core engine is <strong>highly optimized for the Cloudflare environment</strong> (D1 Database and R2 Storage), ensuring blazing-fast, edge-native performance with zero configuration.</p>
                            <p class="mb-3">However, the architecture strictly adheres to a Generic Adapter Pattern to prevent vendor lock-in. If you intend to fork this CMS and deploy it on alternative infrastructure (like Vercel, Netlify, or AWS), you do <strong>not</strong> need to rewrite the UI logic. You only need to modify two central files:</p>
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
                                <li>
                                    <strong class="text-foreground">Database Adapter (<code>src/lib/db.ts</code>)</strong>: 
                                    All 50+ Astro components and API routes import their database connection from this single factory function (`getDb(env)`). To migrate from Cloudflare D1 to PostgreSQL (e.g. Vercel Postgres, AWS RDS), simply swap the Drizzle connection logic inside this single file. The rest of the CMS will automatically inherit the new database engine seamlessly.
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <strong class="text-foreground">Storage Adapter (<code>src/lib/storage.ts</code>)</strong>: 
                                    All media uploads, deletions, and fetch operations across the CMS are routed through the `StorageAdapter` methods (`uploadFile`, `deleteFile`, `listFiles`). To migrate from Cloudflare R2 to standard AWS S3, modify the internal logic of these generic methods in this single file using the AWS SDK. The UI Editor and Media Library will continue functioning flawlessly.
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="bg-muted/30 p-4 rounded-md border border-border mt-4 space-y-3">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Folder Structure & Uploads</h4>
                            <p>If you are exploring the codebase on GitHub, here is the breakdown of the critical directories:</p>
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3">
                                <li><strong><code>/src</code></strong>: Contains all Astro pages, React components, Drizzle DB schema (`src/db`), and layouts.</li>
                                <li><strong><code>/public</code></strong>: Contains static frontend assets (like placeholder images). Files here are served at the root URL `/`.</li>
                                <li><strong><code>/.wrangler</code></strong>: Automatically generated by Cloudflare's Miniflare. Contains your local development D1 database. <em>Do not commit this to GitHub.</em></li>
                                <li class="bg-card/50 p-3 rounded border border-border/50">
                                    <strong class="text-primary text-[15px]">The <code>/local_uploads</code> Directory</strong>
                                    <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">The designated directory for handling media uploads during Local Development.</p>
                                    
                                    <strong class="block mt-3 text-foreground">How it works locally:</strong> 
                                    <p class="mt-1">When you upload an image via the Admin Media Library or TipTap Editor while running <code>npm run dev</code>, the system writes the file directly to <code>/local_uploads</code>. A custom Vite plugin (`serve-local-uploads` in <code>astro.config.mjs</code>) intercepts browser requests to <code>/uploads/*</code> and serves the files straight from this folder. This completely prevents Astro from trying to bundle thousands of images during build time, avoiding massive bloat or build crashes.</p>
                                    
                                    <strong class="block mt-3 text-foreground">When it switches to Cloudflare R2:</strong> 
                                    <p class="mt-1">The system automatically stops using <code>local_uploads</code> when deployed to Production. In the Cloudflare environment, the server-side code detects the presence of the <code>env.UPLOADS</code> R2 binding. Once bound, all media is uploaded directly to the global R2 edge bucket, and the local folder is completely ignored. <em>Note: Do not commit <code>/local_uploads</code> to GitHub.</em></p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 1. Dashboard */}
                <section id="docs-dashboard" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-primary/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-primary/80 group-hover:bg-primary transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Overview: Dashboard</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Serves as the central nerve center for the CMS. It aggregates database counts (Total Articles, Categories, Tags) and provides quick navigation links.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Runs multiple highly concurrent `SELECT COUNT(*)` queries across the `articles`, `categories`, and `tags` tables via Drizzle ORM.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Astro SSR for secure backend aggregation, React (Lucide) for iconography, Tailwind CSS for the responsive grid layout.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction:</strong> Read-only view. No state mutation occurs here. Serves purely as an analytical entry point.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 2. Articles */}
                <section id="docs-articles" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-blue-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-blue-500/80 group-hover:bg-blue-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-blue-500">Content: Articles</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> The core content engine. Allows creation, editing, deletion, and revision tracking of blog posts.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> 
                            The `articles` table is the hub. 
                            - <strong>1-to-Many:</strong> Links to `users` (author) and `categories` (primary category).
                            - <strong>Many-to-Many:</strong> Links to `tags` via the `article_tags` junction table.
                            - <strong>1-to-Many:</strong> Links to `article_revisions` for version control.
                        </p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> React TipTap Editor for headless WYSIWYG, Drizzle ORM for relational mapping, Cloudflare R2 for embedded image signed uploads.</p>
                        
                        <div class="bg-muted/30 p-4 rounded-md border border-border mt-4 space-y-3">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Editor Ecosystem & Automation</h4>
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
                                <li><strong>Image Handling:</strong> A custom floating toolbar (ImageBubbleMenu) allows editors to set Image Alignment (Left, Center, Right), Alt Text, and Caption.</li>
                                <li><strong>Captions & Alt Text:</strong> Transformed dynamically from `title` attributes or `[caption]` shortcodes into HTML5 &lt;figure&gt; and &lt;figcaption&gt; elements. A regex parser automatically catches legacy images with empty `alt=""` and injects the article title as a fallback.</li>
                                <li><strong>FAQ Shortcodes:</strong> A dedicated "?" button generates `[faq]` shortcodes, which are dynamically parsed on the frontend into animated HTML5 &lt;details&gt; Accordion components.</li>
                                <li><strong>Revision History:</strong> Articles automatically snapshot their state before every update. (Note: The `diff` library required for comparing revisions must be excluded from Vite's `optimizeDeps` in `astro.config.mjs` to prevent SSR bundling crashes).</li>
                                <li><strong>Dynamic JSON-LD Schema:</strong> Injects auto-generated `NewsArticle` schema. However, editors can completely override this by pasting raw JSON-LD into the "Custom JSON-LD Schema" textarea in the Editor. The frontend layout instantly detects this column in D1 and overrides the auto-schema.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 3. Pages */}
                <section id="docs-pages" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-emerald-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-emerald-500/80 group-hover:bg-emerald-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-500">Content: Pages</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Manages static standalone routes (e.g., About Us, Contact, Privacy Policy) that exist outside the chronological blog feed.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Uses the `pages` table. Structurally identical to `articles` but lacks taxonomy relationships (no categories or tags). Links 1-to-Many with the `users` table for authorship.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Shares the exact same React TipTap ecosystem as Articles, promoting code reuse.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction:</strong> Rendered dynamically via `/src/pages/[slug].astro` exactly like articles, but omitted from RSS feeds, sitemaps (if excluded), and Frontpage block aggregators. Injects `WebPage` JSON-LD schema.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 4. My Profile */}
                <section id="docs-profile" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-purple-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-purple-500/80 group-hover:bg-purple-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-purple-500">Content: My Profile</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Allows the authenticated user to manage their identity, password, author slug, biography, and social links.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Modifies the `users` table directly. The `socialLinks` column stores an unindexed JSON string to handle arbitrary platform additions without schema migrations.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Uses `bcrypt` (via `jose` or standard Node polyfills depending on environment) to securely hash password updates before D1 insertion.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Editable Fields:</strong> Users can update their Email Address, Password, First Name, Last Name, Display Name, Author URL Slug, Avatar Image (via R2 upload or direct URL), Biography, and Social Links (X, Facebook, Instagram, YouTube, Website 1, Website 2).</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 5. Categories */}
                <section id="docs-categories" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-amber-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-amber-500/80 group-hover:bg-amber-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-500">Content: Categories</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> The primary organizational taxonomy for articles. Hierarchical structurally but currently implemented as a flat list.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> 
                            The `categories` table links to `articles` via the `categoryId` column (1-to-Many). 
                            *Note:* There is also an `article_categories` junction table prepared for future Many-to-Many category scaling.
                        </p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Standard Astro form actions for CRUD operations. Injects `CollectionPage` JSON-LD schema.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction & Routing:</strong> Generates the `/category/[slug]` public routes. Heavily utilized by the Frontpage Builder to dynamically pull content blocks grouped by specific IDs.</p>
                        <div class="bg-amber-500/10 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-4 mt-4 rounded-r-md">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400 mb-1">Architecture Note: Tag Collision</h4>
                            <p class="text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-300">
                                Categories hold taxonomic priority. The system strictly forbids creating a Tag that shares the same name (case-insensitive) as a Category to prevent routing and SEO conflicts.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 6. Tags */}
                <section id="docs-tags" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-rose-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-rose-500/80 group-hover:bg-rose-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-rose-500">Content: Tags</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Micro-taxonomy for granular article grouping (e.g., specific game titles, event names).</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Uses a strict Many-to-Many architecture. The `tags` table connects to `articles` exclusively through the `article_tags` junction table. Safely relies on `ON DELETE CASCADE` to prevent database orphaned record crashes.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Heavy reliance on Drizzle ORM `innerJoin` queries to resolve article lists based on tag slugs.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction & Routing:</strong> Generates the `/tag/[slug]` public routes mimicking legacy WordPress architecture. Includes an inline tag creation API (`/api/tags/create`) built directly into the Article Editor. Due to thousands of tags, the Admin UI uses Vanilla JS fuzzy-search to bypass React rendering overhead.</p>
                        <div class="bg-rose-500/10 border-l-4 border-rose-500 p-4 mt-4 rounded-r-md">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-600 dark:text-rose-400 mb-1">Architecture Note: Tag Creation API & Slug Scrubbing</h4>
                            <p class="text-rose-700 dark:text-rose-300">
                                The `POST /api/tags/create` endpoint executes a strict case-insensitive collision check against the `categories` table. If an editor attempts to create a Tag named "Anime" when an "Anime" Category exists, the API rejects it with a 409 Conflict. Additionally, legacy tags with invalid characters (e.g., Katakana middle dots or uppercase letters) have been scrubbed and permanently converted to strictly lowercase, alphanumeric slugs via a one-time database script.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 7. Media Library */}
                <section id="docs-media" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-cyan-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-cyan-500/80 group-hover:bg-cyan-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-cyan-500">Content: Media Library</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> A central repository for uploading and managing visual assets outside the context of a specific article.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> None directly. Media assets are stateless and exist entirely within the storage bucket. Articles reference them merely by String URL.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Cloudflare R2 bucket (`waritaku-uploads`). Locally, Vite middleware intercepts requests to prevent Astro build bloat.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction:</strong> Uses `env.UPLOADS.list()` to fetch the edge bucket contents and render a visual grid for the admin.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 8. Users & Roles */}
                <section id="docs-users" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-orange-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-orange-500/80 group-hover:bg-orange-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-orange-500">System: Users & Roles</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Manages the identities permitted to access the CMS and defines their exact privilege level.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Modifies the `users` table. Referenced by `articles` and `pages` (as `authorId`) to establish ownership.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Astro locals and middleware (`src/middleware.ts`) intercept every single request to validate JWT session tokens against this table.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Security & Filtering:</strong> Implements a strict two-tier architecture (`admin` vs `superadmin`). The UI conditionally hides features (like System Advanced and General Settings) if the user lacks the `superadmin` string in their role column.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 9. General Settings */}
                <section id="docs-general-settings" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-zinc-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-zinc-500/80 group-hover:bg-zinc-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500">System: General Settings</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Controls the global identity of the website (Site Title, Tagline, Logo, Favicon) and code injections. Access to this panel is strictly limited to users with the `superadmin` role.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Stored as a massive serialized JSON payload in the `settings` table under the `general_settings` key.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> React Hook Form for client-side state management before submitting the JSON payload back to Astro.</p>
                        
                        <div class="bg-muted/30 p-4 rounded-md border border-border mt-4 space-y-3">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Injections & Integrations</h4>
                            <p>Allows appending raw HTML/JS scripts into three strategic layout zones without modifying source files:</p>
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                                <li>`headerHtml`: Injected just before <code>&lt;/head&gt;</code>. Ideal for Google Analytics or external CSS.</li>
                                <li>`footerHtml`: Injected just before <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>. Ideal for tracking pixels or deferred scripts.</li>
                                <li>`articleBottomHtml`: Injected immediately after the article body text in `[slug].astro`. Perfect for global Call-To-Action banners, newsletter signups, or ad placements.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 10. System Advanced */}
                <section id="docs-system-advanced" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-red-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-red-500/80 group-hover:bg-red-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-red-500">System: Advanced System</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Highly destructive / sensitive configurations. Database JSON Exports, API Key storage, and Environment Labeling.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Reads from and writes to the `settings` table (`system_secrets` and `database_environment` keys).</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Cloudflare D1 JSON aggregation (`/api/export-db.ts`) for backups. `env.UPLOADS` binding detection for the Image CDN architecture diagnostic badge.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Security:</strong> Hard-locked to `superadmin`. Data stored here (like OpenAI keys, Telegram bots, Mailgun) are kept strictly out of the frontend client bundles and only accessed via secure SSR API endpoints.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 11. Redirections & 404 Engine */}
                <section id="docs-system-redirects" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-orange-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24 mt-8">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-orange-500/80 group-hover:bg-orange-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-orange-500">System: Redirections & 404 Engine</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Intercepts incoming traffic at the Edge to protect SEO. Tracks broken links in real-time and allows mapping them to permanent 301 redirects.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Uses two dedicated D1 tables: <code>redirects</code> (stores the routing rules) and <code>not_found_logs</code> (stores the broken URLs and hit frequencies).</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> Native Astro Middleware (<code>src/middleware.ts</code>). Runs extremely fast at the Cloudflare Edge before Astro even resolves the requested page route.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Auto-Cleanup:</strong> When an admin creates a redirect rule for a broken link via the Dashboard, the system automatically purges that link from the 404 error logs to maintain a clean UI.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Global Toggles:</strong> Redirection logic and 404 tracking can be globally disabled at any time via <strong>General Settings -&gt; SEO & Edge Routing</strong> to save overhead during massive traffic spikes or development.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 12. Navigation & Footer */}
                <section id="docs-navigation" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-indigo-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-indigo-500/80 group-hover:bg-indigo-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-indigo-500">Appearance: Navigation & Footer</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Dynamic drag-and-drop management of the primary Navbar and Footer link clusters.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Stored as a serialized JSON array of `&#123;label, url, target&#125;` objects in the `settings` table.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> React for the interactive UI management in the admin.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction:</strong> Directly mapped in `src/layouts/LayoutGlobalHeader.astro` and `LayoutGlobalFooter.astro` to render the navigation elements across all frontend pages.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 12. Front Page Builder */}
                <section id="docs-frontpage" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-lime-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-lime-500/80 group-hover:bg-lime-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-lime-500">Appearance: Front Page Builder</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> A modular layout engine allowing editors to stack Hero blocks, Category grids, and Article feeds vertically to design the homepage.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Saves an array of block definitions (e.g., `&#123;type: 'hero_3', limit: 5&#125;`) to the `settings` table (`frontpage_layout`).</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> `index.astro` reads this JSON array and executes massively parallel Drizzle ORM queries using `Promise.all()` to fetch the exact articles required for each block simultaneously.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction:</strong> Dynamically loads corresponding Astro UI components (`NewHeroType3.astro`, `CategoryBlock.astro`) based on the block type strings. The configuration allows dynamic arrangement of blocks like Hero Banners, Category Lists, Article Grids, and Separators.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 13. Global Sidebar */}
                <section id="docs-sidebar" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-fuchsia-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-fuchsia-500/80 group-hover:bg-fuchsia-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-fuchsia-500">Appearance: Global Sidebar</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Controls the right-hand column appearing alongside Articles and Categories.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> JSON array configuration stored in `settings` under `sidebar_widgets`.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> React for administration (`SidebarSettings.tsx`); Astro SSR for rendering (`sidebar.astro`).</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction:</strong> Includes custom HTML injection widgets (e.g. Search, Recent Posts, Custom HTML), making it the primary location for ad-network integrations (AdSense, etc.) without touching source code.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 14. Theme Colors */}
                <section id="docs-theme-colors" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-pink-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-pink-500/80 group-hover:bg-pink-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-pink-500">Appearance: Theme Colors</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Provides GUI controls to modify the foundational HSL color tokens for both Light and Dark modes.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Database Relationships:</strong> Stored as a JSON object of HSL strings in `settings` (`theme_colors`).</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Technologies Used:</strong> The `ThemeInjector.astro` component runs at the absolute top of the DOM <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">UI Interaction:</strong> Overrides Tailwind CSS's default variables globally. A custom FOUC (Flash of Unstyled Content) inline script reads `localStorage` before the body renders, guaranteeing these colors are applied instantly before paint.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 15. SEO & Sitemaps */}
                <section id="docs-seo" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-yellow-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-yellow-500/80 group-hover:bg-yellow-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-yellow-500">Automation: SEO & Sitemaps</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Functionality:</strong> Manages automatic generation of XML sitemaps and dynamic Schema.org markup across all pages.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">XML Sitemap:</strong> A custom SSR endpoint at `/sitemap.xml` dynamically queries the entire D1 database to assemble standard sitemap URLs for all published articles, pages, categories, and tags.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Front Page Schema:</strong> Injects `WebSite` schema with a `SearchAction` definition.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {/* 16. AI & API Discoverability */}
                <section id="docs-ai-api" class="bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden group hover:border-teal-500/50 transition-colors scroll-mt-24">
                    <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1.5 h-full bg-teal-500/80 group-hover:bg-teal-500 transition-colors"></div>
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 border-b border-border/50 pb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-teal-500">Automation: AI & API Discoverability</h3>
                    <div class="space-y-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-sm">
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">Markdown for Agents:</strong> The core routing engine (<code>src/pages/[slug].astro</code>) dynamically intercepts incoming HTTP requests. If a request header includes <code>Accept: text/markdown</code>, the server bypasses Astro HTML rendering entirely. Instead, it utilizes <code>turndown</code> to convert the rich-text content into pure Markdown, injects a YAML frontmatter block (title, tags, date), and returns it directly. This makes all articles and public pages friendly to AI web bots, LLM agents, and terminal tools.</p>
                        <p><strong class="text-foreground">RFC 9727 API Catalog:</strong> To further enhance automated discoverability, the CMS hosts a standardized API Catalog at <code>/.well-known/api-catalog</code> conforming to RFC 9727. It returns an <code>application/linkset+json</code> payload defining relationships (<code>service-desc</code>, <code>service-doc</code>, <code>status</code>) to guide external systems programmatically interacting with the site.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

